```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: aswb1
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the Layer 2 VLAN access configuration on access switch
aswb1 (Office B). The client-facing port eth0 — which connects to the CD_CLIENT_B1
segment — has been left with VLAN 99 as its PVID (Port VLAN ID, Egress Untagged)
instead of the correct user data VLAN 10.

In this topology, VLAN 99 is the management VLAN, carrying the 10.0.0.16/28
management subnet. All other Office B user access switches use VLAN 10 (data,
10.3.0.0/24) plus VLAN 20 (voice/secondary, 10.4.0.0/24) on their client ports
— confirmed on aswb2.eth0 where pc3 works correctly. On aswb1, the bridge VLAN
filter tags every untagged frame arriving on eth0 as VLAN 99 instead of VLAN 10.
A user device plugged into that port is therefore placed entirely on the management
VLAN: it cannot obtain a DHCP lease (no DHCP server exists on VLAN 99), falls back
to APIPA or goes unanswered, and has no path to any user subnet (10.3.0.0/24,
10.4.0.0/24, 10.5.0.0/24 for local office, and 10.1.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24 for
other offices). This fully explains the complaint of "can't reach anything — not
other offices, not even stuff right here in our office."

Root cause: during last night's maintenance, the PVID on aswb1 eth0 was changed
from VLAN 10 to VLAN 99, stranding the connected user device on the management VLAN.

Corrective fix — on aswb1, execute:
  bridge vlan del dev eth0 vid 99 pvid untagged
  bridge vlan add dev eth0 vid 10 pvid untagged
  bridge vlan add dev eth0 vid 20
This restores the eth0 port to match the aswb2 reference configuration (VLAN 10
untagged access + VLAN 20 tagged secondary), immediately restoring user connectivity.
=== END ===
```